  "summary": "Japanese flower arrangement as living sculpture: heaven, earth and humanity held in one stem-balance.",
  "description": "Ikebana arranges plant material by asymmetric line and void: a tall shin (heaven), a supporting soe (earth) and a middle hikae (humanity) composed in measured triangles inside spare containers. Born from Buddhist altar flowers (rikka) in Muromachi temples and refined in tea-room chabana, it split into schools — Ikenobō's rikka discipline, Ohara's landscape moribana in shallow bowls, Sōgetsu's free sculpture. Seasonal materials, negative space and stem-by-stem intention make it closer to calligraphy than decoration.",
